mp-era land deal with a private developer is testing just how far “privatize the national parks” can go — alongside a full breakdown of the eye-watering $2.24 billion Trump has personally profited off crypto, golf resorts, and lawsuit settlements since returning to office, plus Flock surveillance cameras now watching visitors at Yosemite’s gates.\n\nImmigration hits a new record this episode: ICE logged its highest monthly arrest total ever in July 2026, DHS has gone quiet under new leadership, and Faith tells the story of a U.S. Navy sailor’s father — a 19-year U.S. resident with a pending green card — detained despite serving his country. Plus: the U.S. is now deporting people to Liberia under a controversial third-country removal deal, and one Army unit found an unexpectedly effective reenlistment bonus (hint: it involves Grand Theft Auto).\n\nThen, a tribute: Dolly Parton passed away this week at 80, and Faith reflects on why the Deep South icon was one of the rare public figures beloved across the political aisle — her faith, her generosity, her Imagination Library, and her unmatched girl’s-girl energy. From there it’s straight into South Carolina politics, where Lindsey Graham’s sister Darline Graham just won the GOP Senate nomination — and stumbled badly on a basic national security question — setting up a race against Charleston pediatrician and Democratic nominee Annie Andrews.\n\nGood news, sort of: Meta just agreed to a historic $17 billion settlement over Instagram and Facebook’s harm to teens, with real usage limits finally on the table. But it’s followed by a genuinely unsettling one — a federal appeals court just ruled that AI-generated child sexual abuse material may be constitutionally protected under the First Amendment. And in accountability news, former Congressman Eric Swalwell had his devices seized by the FBI amid a sexual assault investigation.\n\nOn the world stage: the CIA director made a rare, unannounced trip to Moscow amid fears Russia could test NATO’s resolve in the Baltics this fall — a callback to the exact playbook Russia ran before invading Ukraine in 2021. Meanwhile, the U.S. and Canada are in a full-blown trade war, with Trump and Ontario Premier Doug Ford trading real insults over tariffs. And overseas, Faith connects the dots between an Israeli-government-funded AI “think tank” secretly shaping chatbot answers about Gaza, a church-targeting propaganda campaign, the one-year anniversary of the Nasser Hospital strike that killed five journalists, and Israel’s new policy treating children’s kites as military threats.\n\nFinally: catastrophic floods in Nepal triggered by a Himalayan glacier collapse, and a deep dive into Gary, Indiana — where a historic Black city was left without power for two weeks after a derecho, and what that says about who gets prioritized in a disaster.","bild_url":"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_nologo/46553019/46553019-1786763956516-49ec8c9b0d4d2.jpg","kapitel_quelle":"modell"}